    "chunk_size_bytes": 1285,
    "chunk_text": "What interests me more than these ideas, however, is Uthpala\u2019s own transformation. While in Kurunegala and even in Colombo, he says he subscribed to certain interpretations of history \u201cbecause they were inscribed in stone and we were supposed to accept them.\u201d What is true of Sri Lanka in this respect is, I think, true of many other countries in South Asia, indeed the Global South. The tendency to associate a country or a society with one group, to the exclusion of others, starts in the classroom and with the textbook.\n\nIn many respects, of course, things have improved since then. Sri Lanka is now in the hands of a government which has, at least on paper, put tolerance and pluralism at the center of its mandate. The country\u2019s prime minister, in particular, is an anthropologist whose work coincides with some of Uthpala\u2019s own research. Yet as Uthpala cautions me as he wraps up our conversation, \u201cour interventions as researchers never end.\u201d\n\nThis is an important point. It is what has informed his work and what has transformed him. On the verge of completing his higher education, Uthpala has made many journeys and crossed many paths. Seeing him grow, I have no doubt that he will continue to evolve. His project, in that sense, may well prove groundbreaking."
  },
