    "chunk_text": "Kerala: Left Falls, Not Out\n\nLate Thursday morning, 10 days after the count, Kerala finally heard the referee\u2019s whistle. Top enablers at Indira Bhawan in New Delhi have finalized the selection of the Chief Minister for Kerala, who will govern for the next five years. AICC General Secretary and the party\u2019s in-charge for Kerala and Lakshadweep, Deepa Dasmunsi, announced that the Congress has chosen V. D. Satheesan, shocking many supporters of K. C. Venugopal. But both have accepted the decision and committed to working together, at least publicly. The episode reflects Kerala\u2019s familiar pattern, where internal competition is settled through central authority and public unity follows factional disputes.\n\nBeneath this stability, the political system is no longer shaped by doctrine but by administration, coalition arithmetic, and tightening fiscal constraints. The Congress-led United Democratic Front (UDF) has returned to power after 10 years in opposition, ending the Communist-led Left Democratic Front (LDF) government. The UDF secured 102 seats in the 140-member Assembly, while the LDF was reduced to 35 seats, and the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) won three, marking a clear reversal of the previous mandate. The communists have fallen, but they are not out. This outcome makes it necessary to revisit Kerala\u2019s Left story."
  },
